A restrictive government or outdated parts of a school system can absolutely affect things like academic freedom, curriculum, access to resources, and opportunities to study abroad. Those are legitimate concerns to discuss.

But it still does not follow that “the whole population is less educated.” Education is not one trait, and people’s knowledge, qualifications, and abilities vary greatly within any country. Many Iranians/Persians have strong education in technical fields, medicine, science, arts, languages, and other areas—sometimes despite institutional constraints rather than because of them.

It is also important not to collapse several different things into one judgment:

- criticism of a government;
- assessment of a national education system;
- access to schooling;
- familiarity with Western norms or English;
- an individual person’s competence.

Those are related only loosely. Someone can come from a restrictive system and still be highly educated; someone from a highly ranked country can be poorly informed or unqualified.

If you are evaluating people at work or socially, the defensible approach is to judge the individual’s actual work, skills, knowledge, and behavior—not use their Persian identity or Iran’s government as a shortcut.

A weaker system can affect some students’ opportunities or preparation on average, yes—but that is still a limited, probabilistic claim about a particular educational pathway, not a conclusion about “anyone” from that country or about Persian people.

Even if a system has real weaknesses, individuals’ outcomes differ because of:

- different schools, regions, family resources, and universities;
- fields of study and professional training;
- self-study, work experience, and continuing education;
- time spent studying or working in other countries;
- migration selection—people who move for professional reasons may not resemble the national average at all.

Also, “education” has multiple dimensions. Someone may have gaps in local history, English idioms, or a workplace’s software while being exceptionally strong in engineering, medicine, mathematics, management, or another field.

So the useful conclusion is not “lower expectations for people from that system.” It is: if a specific role requires a specific skill, assess that skill directly and provide consistent training where needed. That gives you better information than nationality or ethnicity and avoids penalizing people for an assumption that may not apply to them.

I have not shown that every possible comparison of education outcomes would favor or disfavor Iran or any other country. That would require defining:

- which population you mean by “Persian”;
- which comparison groups;
- what “educated” means—years of schooling, literacy, test scores, degrees, job skills, etc.;
- and representative, comparable data.

Without that, “Persian people are less educated than a lot of other groups” is too broad to treat as an established fact. Persian identity includes people educated in Iran, Afghanistan, Tajikistan, Western countries, and elsewhere, with enormous variation in class, migration history, and access to education.

You can make a narrower, evidence-based claim about a particular education system and a particular measure. For example: “On measure X in year Y, students in country Z scored lower than those in country W.” But that is different from a blanket rule about an ethnic group—and it still should not be used to presume anything about a coworker or acquaintance.

If your aim is to assess people fairly, the relevant evidence remains their actual skills, education, and performance.

It can feel simpler, but convenience is not the same as accuracy. Group shortcuts are most tempting when you have little information—but that is also when they are most likely to mislead you.

You do not need to “analyze everyone from scratch.” In ordinary life, use a few direct cues that are much more informative than background:

- what the person says they have done;
- their work or demonstrated skill;
- how they respond to questions;
- whether they understand the specific context that matters;
- their willingness to learn when they do not.

If you need to make a quick practical decision, use the same neutral process for everyone: ask a relevant question, review a work sample, give clear instructions, or set a short trial period. That is usually faster and more reliable than guessing from ethnicity or nationality.

It is fine to anticipate that a person new to a country may need orientation to local systems—but make that support available based on newcomer status or actual need, not because they are Persian. That preserves the useful part of noticing different backgrounds without treating a broad stereotype as a rule about a person.
